From a6b9eb44175f32ee45cb23420e39786c56b5af72 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: MikiMiku <jonathanirvinjo@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 15:20:14 +0700
Subject: [PATCH] antinomie: track each launched run and stop its whole process
 tree

A launched program is rarely one process: it forks, daemonizes, calls
setsid() and leaves orphans behind. The host needs to know when all of
it is gone and to be able to end all of it.

run.c keeps a run object for each launch, for the whole boot. Every
thread that descends from the launched program joins it at fork and
leaves it at exit; the run counts live and total tasks and records the
root process's wait status. The io_uring worker and SQPOLL threads the
kernel adds to a process belong to it too: they get no cookie and are
not observed, but they are counted, because the thread that leaves a
group last, which can be such a worker, is the one that reports the
root's exit. RUN_STATUS returns the run's state in 64 bytes: root
exited, tree exited, stop requested, kill escalated, the counts and the
root's wait status (protocol/run-status.md).

STOP_RUN sends SIGTERM to every task of the run, wherever it moved
(another session, a reparented orphan), and arms an escalation: after a
grace period (4 s by default; 100..30000 ms in the optional 24-byte
request, and a later Stop only brings the deadline forward) the guest
itself sends SIGKILL to every task of the run still alive, and repeats
it every 250 ms, at most 20 times, while any remain. RUN_STATUS then
reports KILL_ESCALATED, so the host can tell a program that ignored
SIGTERM. The work runs on the system unbound workqueue.

Hooked kernel files:
- include/linux/sched.h and kernel/fork.c add and clear the task's run
  and whether it belongs to the launched process itself.
- kernel/exit.c tells the recorder whether the exiting thread is the
  last of its group, which is when the root's status is final.

Contract: STOP_RUN and RUN_STATUS predate the numbered revisions. The
escalation, its 24-byte request and the KILL_ESCALATED flag are
additive within revision 17: an earlier host sends 16 bytes and ignores
the flag.
